WEBVTT


00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:06.400
It can be a challenge to neatly join
seams, especially when you're working in

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.360
stitch patterns.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.720
For this slip stitch sample.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.850
I joined the seam on the machine.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.510
It looks okay a whole lot better than
if I had tried to seam this by hand.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:27.560
All I did was rehang each side seam edge
back on the needles with the public or the

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.360
right sides facing each other.

00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:40.285
Knit one row, then hand knit one loose row

00:00:44.925 --> 00:00:46.995
and bind off stitch through stitch.

00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:51.785
Okay.

00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:56.475
It looks okay, but what about adding
a decorative touch to this seam?

00:00:57.795 --> 00:01:01.035
Hang the first edge with
the public side facing you.

00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:06.445
Then e-Wrap loosely, cast
on over those needles.

00:01:08.445 --> 00:01:13.275
Then hang the other edge, making a
sandwich with the right sides of the work,

00:01:13.275 --> 00:01:14.385
facing each other.

00:01:18.595 --> 00:01:20.065
Then bind off loosely.

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:22.945
Even better.

00:01:22.945 --> 00:01:25.525
Use a double strand of
yarn for the e-Wrap.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.150
Beautiful.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.110
How about using this technique
for a show off shoulder seam!

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.320
Perfect for all over patterns
and especially for fair isle.

